WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:13.260
Good morning Hank; it's Thursday January 24th.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:17.850
Today's blog entry comes in two parts. Part
one: the first part of the survey, part two:

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:18.850
In your pants.

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:21.240
Yeti sock puppet: How long have you known
Hank and Katherine?

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.270
John: Thanks for asking that question, puppet.
I've known Hank since he was born, and I've

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:27.940
known Katherine since, I believe, 1999.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:29.570
Puppet: What do you want from us?

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:33.300
John: You know, puppet, I think that's what
the Buddhists would call a question wrongly

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:39.210
phrased. The question is not, what do I want
from my brother; the question is what does

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:46.579
my brother want from me? And the answer to
that question is, I believe, a Nintendo Wii.

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:48.570
That, that's also what I want from my brother.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:49.570
Puppet: What are you doing?

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:52.949
John: I'm writing books for teenagers and
doing this weird video project with my brother.

00:00:52.949 --> 00:00:54.770
Puppet: Why are you doing it?

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:59.360
John: Well, I write books because it makes
me feel less, like, worthless.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:00.800
Puppet: What's next?

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:06.500
John: Well, the major next at the moment is
finishing my new and as yet untitled third

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:07.500
book.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:08.500
Puppet: How you doin'?

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.990
John: I'm doin really well, sock puppet, and
thank you for asking.

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:13.200
Puppet: What's the best book you've read this
year?

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:18.890
John: Uh, the best book I read last year is
probably The Book Thief by Markus Zusak. Where

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:26.640
should it go? Where? Oh, right beneath his
name, I think. Congratulations, Markus. Definitely

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:27.640
deserved.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:33.149
So, I went around my home library and found
some books that I think benefit from the In

00:01:33.149 --> 00:01:35.960
Your Pants rule, and I'd like to share them
with you.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:39.509
You have Markus Zusak's second novel, Getting
the Girl in Your Pants.

00:01:39.509 --> 00:01:44.320
The unfathomably bad The Mystery of the Golden
Spheres in Your Pants.

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:49.060
Rabbit at Rest in Your Pants. Man, I sure
am glad you stopped hopping.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:50.920
The Cheese Monkeys in Your Pants.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:57.140
Then we have two excellent novels about love
and promiscuity: Everyman in Your Pants, and

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:00.439
Albert Camus's classic The Stranger in Your
Pants.

00:02:00.439 --> 00:02:06.469
The Little Drummer Girl in Your Pants. Ow,
stop drumming, Jesus!

00:02:06.469 --> 00:02:14.760
And of course you have The Sun Also Rises
in Your Pants, which is... which is actually

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.400
what The Sun Also Rises is about.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.320
The Thin Man in Your Pants.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.750
I'm just not gonna say a word.

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:30.370
The Echo Maker in Your Pants. HELLO Hello
hello hello hello. Okay, I never thought I

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:34.650
would do that on the video blog.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:39.400
Everything That Rises Must Converge in Your
Pants.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:44.110
I should add an addendum to the in Your Pants
rule, which is that sometimes "in your pants"

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:49.410
isn't the funniest way that you can add to
a book title. For instance: Will You Miss

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:54.840
Me When I'm Gone in Your Pants is a little
funny, not as funny as Will You Miss Me When

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:59.810
I've Gone in Your Pants, but the funniest
one, I think, is Will You Miss Me When I'm

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:01.870
Gone From Your Pants.

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:04.540
I believe that is an old Carter family tune:

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:16.090
(singing) Will you miss me when I'm gone...
from your pants? Will you miss me when I'm

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:22.810
gone... from your pants? Will you miss me
when I'm go-o-one? Will you miss me when I'm

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:27.880
gone... from your pants?

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:28.780
Hank, I'll see you tomorrow.

